sentences.l:
Heaven got the One, and by it is clear;
earth got the One, and by it is settled;
the spirits got the One, and by it are quick with power;
the valley got the One, and by it is full;
the ten-thousand things got the One, and by it live;
lords and kings got the One, and by it stand as the true measure of all under heaven.
It brings them about:

Earth, with nothing by which to be settled, is like to burst open;
the spirits, with nothing by which to be quick with power, are like to fall silent;
the valley, with nothing by which to be full, is like to run dry;
the ten-thousand things, with nothing by which to live, are like to be snuffed out;
lords and kings, with nothing by which to be honoured and high, are like to topple.
Therefore the honoured takes the base as its root,
the high takes the low as its footing.
Therefore lords and kings style themselves Orphan, Scant, Un-good.
Is this not taking the base as the root? Is it not?
Therefore: carry the counting of a carriage to its end — there is no carriage;
do not want to be glinting like jade,
knobbled like stone.
